# Antichain on Trees generator for arXiv:1705.01240

> **Status:** all local gates pass, but this result is not yet shippable. A bare
> oracle run reached `hardened` at `easy`, but one reply exposed a parser fallback
> bug. After fixing and locally checking that reply, the required clean rerun was
> rejected before inference with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. The
> current transcript preserves those errors; they are not evidence of hardness.

| Profile field | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
| Native domain / object regime | combinatorics / finite discrete |
| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
| Certificate | integer tuple: one representative per colour |
| Native objects | rooted ACT gadget tree; `{0,1,infinity}` placement costs; incomparable assignment |
| Intuition | reduction recognition: root/child exclusions encode a multicoloured clique |
| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
| Reduction | paper-central, Section 3 Lemma 3 and its Appendix proof |

## Problem and trust model

Jones, Lafond, and Scornavacca's [*Consistency of orthology and paralogy constraints
in the presence of gene transfers*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01240) introduces
**Antichain on Trees (ACT)** in its hardness proof. An ACT instance gives a rooted
tree, named elements, and the finite-cost tree nodes available to each element. The
solver must place every element so that no two selected nodes are ancestor-related;
MWACT additionally imposes a total-weight bound.

The module implements the paper's exact Start/Choose/Cover/count construction. It
samples one representative per colour first, constructs a regular compatibility graph
around those representatives, and carries the clique through Lemma 3 into an MWACT
assignment. Generation therefore never solves the instance. The submitted answer is
the paper reduction's compact clique witness. `verify` expands any such candidate into
every ACT placement and exactly checks finite costs, node uniqueness, ancestor
incomparability, and the weight bound without reading `inst["answer"]`.

This is a paper-licensed combinatorial reduction, not native phylogenetic coverage. It
does not hand the solver a relation graph, a gene tree, or an LGT network; Lemma 5
licenses the further reduction to those objects, but this family deliberately stops at
the novel ACT problem the paper itself defines.

## Why Track A is plausible

Lemma 3 reduces `k`-Multicoloured Clique to ACT; Lemma 4 proves ACT NP-hard and MWACT
W[1]-hard even with exactly the `{0,1,infinity}` cost restrictions used here. Lemma 5
then carries that construction to Network Consistency, yielding Theorem 1. The
shipping distribution uses 24 colours of 40 vertices and degree-29 regular bipartite
compatibility graphs, close to the first-moment threshold
`n^k (d/n)^(k choose 2) ~= 1`. Every colour-pair graph is sampled as a thoroughly
switched regular nonedge graph conditioned only on the planted pair remaining an edge;
plants and decoys have identical degrees.

The paper's easy regimes were excluded. Theorem 2 gives `minTransferCost` in
`O(2^k k! k |D| |N|^4)` for maximum least-resolved DS-tree degree `k`, and makes
binary DS-trees polynomial. Theorem 3 makes the unknown-highway, non-minimizing
variant polynomial. This generator instead uses the unbounded-degree Lemma 3 image.
The paper's worst-case result alone does not establish hardness of this planted
distribution: the evidence is the adversary panel. Its domain-standard exact
multicoloured-CSP solver (MRV plus forward checking) exhausted 2,000,001 nodes on all
8 audit seeds, averaging 37,830,076 compatibility updates and 21.10 seconds under the
shared audit CPU.

## Worked demo

`make_instance(n=3, classes=3, degree=2, seed=7)` renders the following complete
problem. A person can solve it on paper by testing at most 27 triples.

```text
Find a bounded-weight incomparable assignment in an Antichain on Trees instance.

Definitions.
A rooted-tree node a is an ancestor of b when a lies on the path from the
root to b; a node is its own ancestor. Two nodes are incomparable when
neither is an ancestor of the other. An MWACT assignment maps every named
element to one finite-cost tree node, all mapped nodes are pairwise
incomparable, and the integer costs sum to at most the stated bound.

This instance uses exactly the following finite gadget grammar. Every gadget
root and every COUNT_SINGLETON node is a child of one global root. Leaves
listed for a gadget are its direct children. Distinct gadgets have no other
ancestor relation.

  START has root S_IN and leaves CLASS_IN(i).
  CHOOSE(v) has root V_IN(v), leaf CLASS_OUT(i,v) for v in colour i,
    and leaf TO_OUT(u,i,v) whenever u in another colour is compatible with v.
  COVER(v), for v in colour i, has root V_OUT(v), leaf COUNT_IN(v),
    and leaves TO_IN(v,j) for every j different from i.
  COUNT_SINGLETON(i) is a one-node gadget COUNT_OUT(i).

The elements and all their finite placements are:
  START -> S_IN at cost 0.
  CLASS(i) -> CLASS_IN(i) at cost 0, or CLASS_OUT(i,v) at cost 1.
  VERTEX(v) -> V_IN(v) at cost 0, or V_OUT(v) at cost 1.
  COUNT(v), v in colour i -> COUNT_IN(v) at cost 0, or COUNT_OUT(i) at cost 1.
  TO(v,j) -> TO_IN(v,j) at cost 0, or TO_OUT(v,j,u) at cost 1 for a
    compatible u in colour j. All unlisted placements have infinite cost.

There are 3 colours, each with 3 graph-vertex labels. The weight bound
is K=15. Colour indices below are 1-based.

A short witness is required. Give one representative v_i from each colour i.
It expands deterministically as follows: START uses S_IN; CLASS(i) uses
CLASS_OUT(i,v_i); VERTEX(v_i) uses V_OUT(v_i), COUNT(v_i) uses COUNT_OUT(i),
and TO(v_i,j) uses TO_OUT(v_i,j,v_j). Every corresponding element for an
unchosen v uses its cost-0 IN node. The checker performs this full expansion
and checks every finite placement, tree incomparability, and the weight bound.

Compatibility matrices are given for each colour pair i<j. Rows follow the
printed order of colour i and bit positions follow the printed order of
colour j. A 1 means compatible and a 0 means incompatible. Positions are
1-based in this explanation. After each PAIR label, slash-separated bit
strings are consecutive rows; decimal vertex labels themselves are arbitrary.

Colour classes (in answer-slot order):
  C1: 6 8 4
  C2: 9 5 3
  C3: 7 1 2

Compatibility matrices:
  PAIR C1 C2: 011/101/110
  PAIR C1 C3: 110/011/101
  PAIR C2 C3: 011/101/110

Output exactly 3 distinct decimal labels as a JSON list. Entry i must
belong to colour i in the printed order. No repeats are allowed; list order
is significant; all label and index bounds are inclusive.

Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that JSON list.
Example syntax: <answer>[6,9,7]</answer>
Output nothing else inside the tags.
```

The planted answer is `<answer>[8,9,1]</answer>`. The exact checks give:

```python
verify(inst, [8, 9, 1])
# (True, "ok")
verify(inst, [6, 9, 1])
# (False, "representatives 6 and 9 are incompatible")
```

## Presets and gates

The intended shipping rung is `easy`; `medium` and `hard` only enlarge each colour
class, keeping the answer at 24 labels and the intended route at 276 pair checks.

